WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.034
Python tracebacks are valuable during development because they show where
failure occurred and how execution reached that point.

2
00:00:07.034 --> 00:00:18.376
They can include file paths, module names, function names, line numbers,
framework internals, dependency behavior, variable representations, and
sometimes sensitive values embedded in exception messages.

3
00:00:18.376 --> 00:00:24.839
That detail helps developers fix issues, but it is rarely appropriate for
production users or unauthenticated clients.

4
00:00:24.839 --> 00:00:29.972
Debug mode and interactive debugger features are particularly sensitive in
web applications.

5
00:00:29.972 --> 00:00:37.892
Development tools may show exception pages, local variables, environment
details, request data, configuration hints, or interactive controls.

6
00:00:37.892 --> 00:00:43.342
Those features are intended for trusted local diagnostics, not public or
semi-public environments.

7
00:00:43.342 --> 00:00:51.072
Production systems should use safe defaults that prevent raw tracebacks
and interactive debugging interfaces from reaching untrusted users.

8
00:00:51.072 --> 00:00:54.938
Environment separation keeps diagnostic behavior in its proper place.

9
00:00:54.938 --> 00:00:58.803
Local development can be verbose because the audience is the developer.

10
00:00:58.803 --> 00:01:04.822
Staging may need more detail than production, but it can still contain
sensitive data or external integrations.

11
00:01:04.822 --> 00:01:15.658
Production should send limited responses while preserving controlled
internal diagnostics in logs, traces, crash reports, or error reporting
systems with appropriate access control and redaction.

12
00:01:15.658 --> 00:01:19.967
Framework-provided error handlers are helpful when configured
deliberately.

13
00:01:19.967 --> 00:01:26.050
Flask, Django, FastAPI, ASGI servers, worker frameworks, and task queues
each have ways to shape error handling.

14
00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:30.992
The durable principle is to verify the deployed behavior, not just assume
defaults are safe.

15
00:01:30.992 --> 00:01:39.800
Production-like tests should confirm that raw tracebacks, debug pages,
unsafe headers, and internal exception text are not exposed through normal
failure paths.
